.TH LUS-CHECK 1 "" "Lus" "Lus Manual"
.SH NAME
lus-check \- find errors and likely mistakes without running the code
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBlus check\fR [\-\-json] [\-\-fix] [\-\-strict] [\-\-list] [\-\-explain code] [path ...]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Finds errors and likely mistakes without running the code.
It parses, lints, and compiles each file, then prints each problem as \fBfile:line:column: severity: message [code]\fR.
For a directory, it checks every \fB.lus\fR file inside it.
Files named \fB*_test.lus\fR are checked with the conventions of \fBlus test\fR.
The exit status is 1 when an error or a warning is reported.
Hints alone leave it at 0.
Checking lists the diagnostics.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print the diagnostics as one JSON array, for editors and other tools.
.TP
\fB\-\-strict\fR
Also report where a file departs from the strict style and report as warnings the hints that name a Lus form of a Lua pattern.
.TP
\fB\-\-list\fR
Print every diagnostic code with its severity and summary.
.TP
\fB\-\-fix\fR
Apply the rewrites that keep what the program does, write the changed files, then report what remains.
Fixes lists the rewrites.
.TP
\fB\-\-explain code\fR
Print why a diagnostic code matters and how to resolve it, with an example that reports the code and the same example fixed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
No error or warning was reported.
.TP
1
An error or a warning was reported.
.TP
2
An option is malformed or a file cannot be read.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lus (1)
.PP
The CLI page of the Lus manual, https://lus.dev/manual/cli#lus-check, has the same text with links to the rest of the manual.
